#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"}] :question/id #uuid "87b69a53-94f5-51e6-9c3a-e403abbf1fa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21674" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4x(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[2, \\ 0]^T?$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/37/3778afde5f90cb0f7d0ac6cfe75a38e790d3c29ac5b16148ff997bc5e2d618b6.png) and \n![](images/bd/bda68b8ced804286f348d4ba16e62fd98461be02c9a93974fa328f96fc650597.png)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/ed/edc462937a0fd6be62bc13a9ef9ec02640c374eec382e5f0a69b08c08ef1b72f.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/7a/7a5e0413bd5830d067ee908e054f4e8f9949a464ea93587fc9a0b61897f7c420.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/e4/e42f71d062ff514ebcb1c2abefea9d4174896d4b67f52da8a67c99c2e79a4d24.png) to get\n\n![](images/8f/8ffda98648f540450a8071dda6f20208768e35c7f0858936f5e17379381c00dd.png)\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n![](images/11/1153c649732a1fad021e0118b5779e55f3b18de5958479088343770d1b8f95bc.png)\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n![](images/43/4369f0ba7e84c6e3a9fc540b1395fdb74ee74eb50d895fac0428dfb9cd8abe27.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "0f4ae74c-67d0-5c0e-99db-e834253b3851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21675" :question/problem "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/58/58d027aeb94fddf42231a86a73de4be306fa6ea22ea7767dbe8a461e91a6f446.png) and \n![](images/bb/bb9297479f2149dfca76e02fb9e314205063fd3a3f967d70eee24235970206b1.png)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ea02cc0d71b67f061adb67ee344cc5474b71e1e34db122b942d2e0ed1531ae8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/8f/8f1e68dd09715b24f0de6c79fdf07af6e9810741e1a6065637db5301cd86ebe0.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/ef/ef4a060144a80ac9ecb94a71c407d1537283ecc217ac26ab053e9b8d2fad73e9.png) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)-(0)(0) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{almost linear}}$ at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"}] :question/id #uuid "fd2d2d61-53a6-51c1-811c-41393270ddba" :question/math-academy-id "e-21676" :question/problem "![](images/45/45f0e29c3f9f2c90418de2d78f15dedef030364d08d0dfd7d59fdafc6b58ae5f.png)\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system’s solutions near the equilibrium ![](images/c1/c1049cbc5a1c2cd49fe8504e44ef376c53acf2fe06d0c3a06782401d2051db71.png)" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n![](images/66/665073929c173cf7c3203e3db1044e266e78d99aad05931a0a7aa14879ecd816.png)\nwhere ![](images/e7/e79cd0cff5e7c83a4f69c803ff874d83922a812f3d1b03c7493844e5412239b7.png) and \n![](images/48/48201bab65eff0afe75ab57b82923b1154ab75cdd89c0af89199c8638b9a78c2.png)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of ![](images/72/72080776eb0484de4d6ce7ac1948f97707f8927a53682b96499071e9adf62061.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/00/00f93cbfbbba02eb849ac615aa42842f8b163a833e21e2be6fd091f3f0c1ee61.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n![](images/16/1616c48bd661199c6ba7a1804bdfda5b93093585a93899b3f8c716603c90902d.png)\n\nThus, we have:\n\n![](images/55/550836e05a8d71cddc6c1e7d7dd8f640b32f7d02d03c34f322376f1d7fb434b4.png)\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/41/41900455148f5799343ca024ae556b4bcc9a55ee88518c4a6ed39b3fa5e63f76.png) to get\n\n![](images/3b/3b531a0ee22bdb4c79db12c53286ea7f5a8fce3b25d8f2f2ee9088156706f008.png)\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ![](images/49/49123a99ea93efe4f95823ac19286162e9e252def806ee1633bea2bffc15f1e8.png)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n![](images/ea/ea281be2b31f2cad60203b3ef3c17208ef03593aaa2ef15442b811989c429a07.png)\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues\n![](images/ea/ea394775ef6cd0adf8425800baac86779b92856950a4016668433687aef524c6.png)\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an ![](images/4e/4e3952dffb6f2a5cd730e0f013eb0b49f49ce3be9a2e83674272991baaf57225.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6634ee-910f-55f8-9d60-5c8c995940b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368110} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4f5267-b311-5097-b0ef-bfed5c3b88c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6634ee-910f-55f8-9d60-5c8c995940b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368110} :answer-field/id #uuid "decc9c2a-219a-5a8c-88e2-22756060d4c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4abcfb-223d-5381-849b-3fb4ca82f7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368113} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c1586e-ee5f-57ad-89b4-73c090ce68fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368114} {:answer/id #uuid "c830c193-7c1a-5c97-8afa-819b0b740a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368115} {:answer/id #uuid "032b476e-9204-5a08-8fd0-f04b6ce96683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b476e-9204-5a08-8fd0-f04b6ce96683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368116} :answer-field/id #uuid "734e541f-2137-5488-88d1-d745fa7b5050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b642f63-61e2-5bfd-b745-caa14baed777" :question/math-academy-id "q-334949" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b70951-c3a2-5027-8362-5049ab69c579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220705} {:answer/id #uuid "d88af9a5-f095-5c98-a8c6-e0cb1708d875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220706} {:answer/id #uuid "e0360109-5f89-58c1-ac04-73544cab17d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}- π & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220707} {:answer/id #uuid "02c12be6-707d-59b0-bd5b-7b6cba412226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & π \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220708}] :answer-field/id #uuid "abc8c239-4587-5843-b6eb-bae3745751a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1bbbcd1-b6c5-5bbb-8191-f58755803375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220710} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9b1914-b734-5f05-9c5e-cae54a2da290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220711}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef8de12-13b2-5183-a215-bd0ab012915a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72340dc1-5dda-5425-8945-6d48e1143d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368118} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecdaede-59ca-5ab4-a488-3865e7146c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368119} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf12431-0a5a-53d0-8213-0b17195297a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368120} {:answer/id #uuid "aada5db9-0588-53a6-8255-685956e45ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecdaede-59ca-5ab4-a488-3865e7146c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368119} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c3dfe3-4874-5366-8605-975305c9e601" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "550f465e-0ee4-5206-960f-79ab7b2721a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368123} {:answer/id #uuid "b099dcf5-d989-5208-bec1-e4d448a6144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b099dcf5-d989-5208-bec1-e4d448a6144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368124} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e06f99-af82-5f1c-a3a7-0a32284d0555" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c191cb5-1a9c-5ce5-b394-bfa06fef9db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334954" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca75dc5-e0b6-5c02-874f-e33f31f85c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368127} {:answer/id #uuid "32a0ded4-8d51-595f-b54e-b33b7a306ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368128} {:answer/id #uuid "51d90af4-54c9-5128-ba35-9b467a03fa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368129} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2bef84-c1de-5272-bda5-a317f440d239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a0ded4-8d51-595f-b54e-b33b7a306ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368128} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f025dfa-239c-52e6-a0d5-ea0f34a73cff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1058622f-c1d4-5dc2-abb0-cc58e5a6fbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368132} {:answer/id #uuid "77a437ce-d845-5e56-85ee-f105ed7b6adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77a437ce-d845-5e56-85ee-f105ed7b6adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368133} :answer-field/id #uuid "eab15078-c9f6-501d-9cc8-a86c89e80717" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "267b0a4d-f99c-548c-bed7-e5ca46d9be25" :question/math-academy-id "q-335103" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & \\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & \\pi (0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & 2(0)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae2533e-427c-5750-ae66-88d1e4378acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae2533e-427c-5750-ae66-88d1e4378acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368186} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d25b9b-5373-5c00-8a36-5090fe82791f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368185} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c5fc6a0-19fe-56a4-9cf0-1de76c6493f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c5fc6a0-19fe-56a4-9cf0-1de76c6493f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368188} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c29b406-8180-595f-9aae-3d178f19a2c1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af767bc3-d9be-53cf-8407-19d1ce259c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af767bc3-d9be-53cf-8407-19d1ce259c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368190} :answer-field/id #uuid "66e37ca5-6012-50f9-89f3-865526e6acc9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320daf72-7d61-5223-a594-9af115706e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320daf72-7d61-5223-a594-9af115706e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368192} :answer-field/id #uuid "0090cd01-6fbb-5a08-8dac-f73a47491295" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764d1d84-2f88-5b8f-983f-958d465089bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-336904" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)y(t)-2x(t)+3y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)+4y(t)+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x+4y+4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5)=2x+y-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5)=x+3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-x+4y+4)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-x+4y+4)=2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2 & x+3 \\\\ -1 & 2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)+(-1)-2 & 1+3 \\\\ -1 & 2(-1)+4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
